      "textEn": "60\nThey think that they are independent from God or that they consider themselves one of the three independent incarnations.\nThey believe. Brahma has been assigned the task of creating the universe, Vishnu of sustaining it, and Rudra or Shivji of destroying it.\nThe task has been assigned. These three are considered incarnations of God, who are the preservers of three different qualities,\nbut none of these three is independent of God. Here, that's why\nthe word 'difference' has come, because Brahmaji thinks that they are independent like Rudra. Sometimes Brahmaji considers himself independent, and his worshippers also consider Brahmaji\nindependent. Therefore, after the destruction of this world, when the mutual action of the three qualities\ncauses creation again, Brahma returns. Although Brahmaji, the first incarnation of Mahavishnu,\nreaches him, but he cannot stay in Vaikuntha.\n\nThe special importance of his return is worth seeing.\nBrahma, the great sages, and the great of yoga,\nSwami (Shiva) are not ordinary beings; they are all extremely powerful and complete with all the yogic perfections. Even then, they want to be one with the Supreme Brahman, therefore they have to come back.\nIs. In Shrimad Bhagwat, it is accepted that as long as a person considers himself equal to God,\nhe remains completely pure or knowable. After the destruction, such people, the first\nman incarnation, even after going to Mahavishnu, return to this world again.\n\nThis thinking of the non-absolutists is their terrible downfall that God is within the physical body\nthere are types, therefore there is no need to contemplate the form of God, but one should contemplate the formless\nshould contemplate. Due to this mistake, even the greatest yogis or spiritualists return after creation. Besides the Nirvisheshvadis and Advaitavadis, all beings, being complete,\nlive in Krishna consciousness, perform devotion, and become liberated by generating divine love for God.\nSuch devotion, when one thinks of God as master, friend, son, and lover, respectively,\nis produced. Such differences will always be present."
