      "textEn": "603\nThey are the three classes of men, the lord of the three classes, the destroyer of the three classes. Of the story, the heroic deeds of the hero, the honey-devouring one. 18. Shabdarman\n\nTraya-Bargika:--One who is interested in the three progressive methods; Te--They; Purusha--Individual; Vimukha--Not interested;\nHari-Medhasa:--Of Lord Hari; Kathayaam--In stories; Kathaniya--To be narrated; Uru-Vikramasya--Of whom\nThe best Vikram ( valor ); Madhu-Dwisha--Slayer of Madhu Asura;..\n\nSuch people are called triadic, because they are interested in three progressive methods of endeavor. They turn away from that God, who is the one who grants rest to the bound souls. They do not show any interest in those divine plays of God, which are worth listening to because of the divine prowess of God. Meaning: According to Vedic ideology, there are four progressive principles (Purusharthas), whose names are Dharma, Artha, Kama, and Moksha. Those who are interested in material pleasures, they keep doing their assigned work.\nThey are interested in three progressive methods – dharma, artha and kama. By improving their\neconomic condition, they can enjoy material life. As a result, materialistic\npeople are only interested in those progressive methods that are called tri-vartik. Trai ka\nMeans three and vargik ka means \"Improving methods\".\" Such men are never attached to God,\nbut rather they are against him. Here, Lord is called Hari-Mekha: which means “one who liberates from the cycle of birth and death”.\"A materialistic person never shows any curiosity to listen to the miraculous plays of God. They consider these to be fabricated stories and God\nto be a physical man. They are not fit to progress in devotion or Krishna consciousness. Such people\nshow interest in stories published in newspapers, ITE and fictional plays.\nGod\nThe real activities of Shri Krishna, such as the activities of Lord Kurukshetra or\nthe activities of the Pandavas, the description of the Leelas of Lord Vrindavan or Dwarka is in Bhagavad Gita\nand Srimad Bhagavat, which are full of the description of the Leelas of God. But\nMaterialistic men are busy elevating their position in the world and such a God"
